('p','The Query Editor opens with the table listed under Selected Datasets on the left and an empty worksheet on the right. Type or paste a statement into the **SQL Query** box, leave **Run Options** at Preview, and click **Run**.'),
('note','Important','The worksheet accepts one statement at a time and rejects a trailing semicolon. It also defaults to a preview limit of 1,000 rows. Aggregates are unaffected, but if a plain `SELECT` seems to stop at 1,000 rows, the limit is why.'),
('code','SELECT count(*) AS subscribers,\n       sum(churned_next_30d) AS churned,\n       round(avg(churned_next_30d), 4) AS churn_rate\nFROM   dataengineeringlab.public.subscribers'),
('figure','shots/03_query_editor.png','The Query Editor with the base-rate query entered. Run executes it.'),
('p','The query appears under **Query Results** with a Status of Succeeded and its duration. Click the arrow at the left of the row to expand the result rows, or **View All** for the full history.'),
('figure','shots/03b_query_results.png','Query Results. Each statement you run is listed with its status; expand a row to see the values.'),
('p','Expected: 200000 subscribers, 23796 churned, churn rate 0.119. Roughly 12 percent is the base rate, and every later number is only meaningful compared with it. Run each of the following statements the same way.'),
('h3','Step 2.3 - Does the plan matter?'),
('code','SELECT plan, count(*) AS people, round(avg(churned_next_30d), 4) AS churn_rate\nFROM   dataengineeringlab.public.subscribers\nGROUP  BY plan ORDER BY churn_rate DESC'),
('table',['plan','people','churn_rate'],[['basic','89977','0.1526'],['standard','70123','0.0973'],['premium','39900','0.0812']],'Churn rate by plan'),
('p','Basic churns nearly twice as often as premium. Real signal.'),
('h3','Step 2.4 - Do support tickets matter?'),
('code','SELECT support_tickets_90d, count(*) AS people, round(avg(churned_next_30d), 4) AS churn_rate\nFROM   dataengineeringlab.public.subscribers\nGROUP  BY support_tickets_90d HAVING count(*) > 200\nORDER  BY support_tickets_90d'),
('table',['tickets','people','churn_rate'],[['0','141001','0.0986'],['1','49185','0.1536'],['2','8635','0.2249'],['3','1069','0.3265']],'Churn rate by support tickets in the last 90 days'),
('p','Every complaint roughly compounds the risk. This is the clearest relationship in the data.'),
('h3','Step 2.5 - Does tenure matter?'),
('code',"SELECT CASE WHEN tenure_months <=  6 THEN '1. 0-6 mo'\n            WHEN tenure_months <= 12 THEN '2. 7-12 mo'\n            WHEN tenure_months <= 24 THEN '3. 13-24 mo'\n            WHEN tenure_months <= 36 THEN '4. 25-36 mo'\n            ELSE '5. 37+ mo' END AS tenure_band,\n       count(*) AS people, round(avg(churned_next_30d), 4) AS churn_rate\nFROM   dataengineeringlab.public.subscribers\nGROUP  BY 1 ORDER BY 1"),
('table',['tenure band','people','churn_rate'],[['0-6 months','20213','0.2070'],['7-12 months','20597','0.1826'],['13-24 months','40506','0.1484'],['25-36 months','41077','0.1097'],['37+ months','77607','0.0687']],'Churn rate by tenure band'),
('p','New customers churn three times more than long-standing ones, and the relationship is monotonic. Bucketing a number into bands is a real analyst move: month by month is noise, bands show the shape.'),
('h3','Step 2.6 - Does country matter?'),
('code','SELECT country, count(*) AS people, round(avg(churned_next_30d), 4) AS churn_rate\nFROM   dataengineeringlab.public.subscribers\nGROUP  BY country ORDER BY churn_rate DESC'),
('table',['country','people','churn_rate'],[['DE','24148','0.1211'],['US','60091','0.1197'],['IN','49988','0.1191'],['BR','29947','0.1184'],['JP','15838','0.1172'],['GB','19988','0.1162']],'Churn rate by country'),
('p','Six countries, every one within half a percent of the base rate. Country predicts nothing. It was placed in the data as deliberate noise. Germany looks worse than Britain only until you notice that every other column varies by two to three times and this one varies by half a percent.'),
('h3','Step 2.7 - Prove it with one number'),
('code','SELECT round(max(r) - min(r), 4) AS spread\nFROM  (SELECT avg(churned_next_30d) AS r\n       FROM   dataengineeringlab.public.subscribers\n       GROUP  BY country)'),
('p','Expected spread for country: 0.0049. Change `GROUP BY country` to `GROUP BY plan` and the spread becomes 0.0715, fifteen times wider. A useless column is not one that scores zero. It is one whose categories are indistinguishable from each other. The same fingerprint reappears in the model\'s feature importance in Part 7.'),
('h3','Step 2.8 - Two sources in one statement'),
('code','SELECT (SELECT count(*) FROM dataengineeringlab.public.subscribers) AS subscribers_in_postgres,\n       (SELECT count(*) FROM system.runtime.nodes)                  AS query_engine_nodes'),
('p','The first catalog is a Postgres database on another machine. The second is the query engine\'s own internal catalog. Two different systems, one `SELECT`, no copy of either. That is federation, and swapping `system` for a second real database changes nothing about how the query is written.'),

