('p','The notebook opens in a new tab. Run the cells in order from the top: select a cell and click the **Run** button in the notebook toolbar, or press Shift+Enter. To run everything at once use **Kernel, Restart Kernel and Run All Cells**. Later cells use variables defined by earlier ones, so skipping ahead fails with a `NameError`.'),
('figure','shots/09_notebook_open.png','The lab notebook open in JupyterLab, with the Run button in the toolbar.'),
('h3','Part 1.1 - Check the environment'),
('p','The first cell inspects the installed packages and prints `environment ready` when they match the required set. On a correctly built image it installs nothing. If it has to install, it says so and tells you to restart the kernel, which is unavoidable because NumPy is compiled into other packages. cuDF, cuML and cuVS are reported but never installed by the notebook. They must be baked into the image.'),
('code','numpy 2.4.6 · pandas 3.0.3 · cuDF present · cuML · cuVS\n\nenvironment ready — no restart needed, carry on to 1.2'),
('h3','Part 1.2 - Set your student number'),
('p','This is the only cell you edit. Everything else, which folder you read, what your experiment is called, what your model is named, is built from this number.'),
('numbers',[
 'In the code cell, find the line `STUDENT_ID = 0`.',
 'Replace the `0` with your own student number, without leading zeros. Student 7 types `7`.',
 'Run the cell.',
]),
('figure','shots/09c_student_id.png','Part 1.2. Replace the value after STUDENT_ID with your own student number before running the cell.'),
('code',cell('## 1.2')),
('p','The cell prints your zero-padded number, the curated folder it will read, and the feature window, then confirms the folder exists.'),
('code','You are student 07\nReading from : /home/<user>/shared/data-engineering-lab/curated/student-07/watch_events\nFeature window: events on or after 2026-05-31\n\n✅ Your curated data is there — carry on.'),
('p','If the check fails, either the Spark job has not finished or the number does not match the one used in Stage 3.'),
('h3','Part 1.3 - GPU check and memory cap'),
('p','The cell initialises the RAPIDS memory manager with a 1.5 GB pool cap and prints the GPU. Everything in the lab fits comfortably inside that cap, and on a shared card it is what protects the other users.'),
('code',cell('## 1.3')),
('code','✅ cuDF 26.08.01 available — GPU memory capped at 1.5 GB\n\nname, memory.total [MiB], memory.used [MiB]\nNVIDIA L40S, 46068 MiB, 1234 MiB'),
('h3','Part 2 - What Spark produced'),
('p','Only the last 30 day folders are read. The other 150 are never opened. That is partition pruning.'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 2')),
('code','day-folders Spark wrote   : 180\nday-folders we will read  : 30   ← the other 150 are never opened\nparquet files to read     : 30'),
('h3','Part 3 - The squash, CPU then GPU'),
('p','The same function rolls every subscriber\'s last 30 days into one row: total minutes, number of sessions, completion rate and distinct titles. It runs first with pandas on the CPU, then with cuDF on the GPU. The function does not change. Only the library that read the data does.'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 3')),
('code',cell('## Now the same thing on the GPU')),
('p','Expected: 3,286,532 rows read, 196,564 people out, and a squash speed-up line comparing the two timings. On the reference L40S run the CPU squash took 0.39 seconds and the GPU squash 0.03 seconds, a 12 times speed-up. The GPU read includes a one-time start-up cost, so the squash time is the honest comparison.'),
('figure','shots/10_part3.png','Part 3 output on the GPU. The same aggregation as the CPU cell, with the measured speed-up and the first rows of the squashed table.'),
('note','Important','The squash produces 196,564 people, not 200,000. The remaining 3,436 subscribers watched nothing in the last 30 days and therefore have no row here. Part 5 is where they come back.'),
('h3','Part 4 - The label lives somewhere else'),
('p','The cell reads the 200,000 subscriber rows from Postgres with a read-only account: plan, country, payment method, price, tickets, tenure and the churn label. Expected: 200,000 rows and a churn rate of 0.1190.'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 4', max_lines=24)),
('h3','Part 5 - Join the two halves on the GPU'),
('p','Behaviour meets identity on `subscriber_id`. The join is a **left** join from subscribers with missing activity filled as zero, so the 3,436 silent subscribers are kept.'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 5')),
('p','Expected: a 200,000 by 12 training table, and a two-row summary showing that people who churned watched about 205 minutes against about 577 for people who stayed. That gap is what the model will learn.'),
('h3','Part 5b - The validation checklist, executed'),
('p','Before anything is trained, the cell runs nine checks that mirror the rows of the transformation and validation checklist: join completeness, key uniqueness, nulls, value ranges, the label base rate, the zero-activity group, and equality of the CPU and GPU aggregations. It stops the notebook if any check fails.'),
('code','✅ PASS  [3.2] LEFT join kept every subscriber    200,000 in, 200,000 out\n✅ PASS  [3.4] join key unique on both sides      subscriber_id unique\n✅ PASS  [3.3] no nulls after fill                0 nulls\n✅ PASS  [3.5] zero-activity group preserved      3,436 people watched nothing; 63% of them churned\n✅ PASS  [2.5] completion_rate within [0, 1]      max 1.000\n✅ PASS  [2.5] counts non-negative                ok\n✅ PASS  [0.5] label base rate near 0.12          0.1190\n✅ PASS  [3.6] churners watch less than stayers   mean minutes {0: 577, 1: 205}\n✅ PASS  [2.4] CPU and GPU squash identical       196,564 rows, same total minutes\n\nALL PASS — this table is ready to train on.'),
('figure','shots/11_part5b.png','Part 5b. Every transformation validated before training.'),
('p','The line for the zero-activity group is the most important number in the lab. Sixty-three percent of the people who watched nothing churned, against twelve percent overall. An inner join would have deleted them, and with them 2,172 churners, without any error.'),

('h2','Stage 5 - Train, explain, register'),
('h3','Part 6 - Train the model'),
('p','Words become numbers by one-hot encoding plan, country and payment method, giving 19 features. A stratified split keeps 25 percent hidden. `device=\'cuda\'` puts XGBoost on the GPU with no other change.'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 6')),
('p','Expected: a test AUC near 0.87. AUC is the probability that a randomly chosen churner scores higher than a randomly chosen non-churner. A value of 0.5 is a coin flip, and anything near 1.0 should make you suspect a leak.'),
('h3','Part 7 - Which columns mattered'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 7')),
('p','Two things to look for. Columns that measure the same underlying thing share the credit, so `minutes_30d` ranks low even though churners watch a third as much; importance shows what the model used, not what the world contains. And the six `country_*` columns land within 0.002 of each other while the real features differ by ten times. Real features disagree with each other. Noise features are indistinguishable.'),
('code','TOP 8 FEATURES\ndistinct_titles_30d       0.387297\nsessions_30d              0.339300\nsupport_tickets_90d       0.046647\npayment_method_prepaid    0.029106\nmonthly_price             0.028554\ntenure_months             0.021727\ncountry_BR                0.014328\nminutes_30d               0.014240\n\nTHE PLANTED NOISE (country)\ncountry_BR    0.014328\ncountry_DE    0.013573\ncountry_GB    0.013206\ncountry_US    0.012810\ncountry_IN    0.012603\ncountry_JP    0.012573\n\n  noise columns : spread 0.0018  (from 0.0126 to 0.0143)\n  real  columns : spread 0.3873  (from 0.0000 to 0.3873)'),
('figure','shots/12_part7.png','Parts 6 and 7. The held-out AUC and the feature importance with the noise fingerprint.'),
('h3','Part 8 - Register the model in MLflow'),
('p','The cell logs the four hyperparameters, the AUC, a set of tags and the model itself, and registers it as `student-NN-churn`. The platform token is read automatically from the notebook environment.'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 8', max_lines=18)),
('code','Successfully registered model \'student-07-churn\'.\n✅ Registered as: student-07-churn\n   AUC 0.8712\nCreated version \'1\' of model \'student-07-churn\'.'),
('figure','shots/13a_part8_output.png','Part 8 output. The warnings are expected. Running the cell again creates the next version of the same registered model.'),
('h3','Step 5.1 - Find your run in MLflow'),
('numbers',[
 'In the left navigation choose **Tools & Frameworks**, find the **MLflow** tile and click **Open**. MLflow opens in a new tab.',
 'On the **Experiments** page, type your student number in the search box on the left, for example `student-07`, and click the experiment `student-NN-churn`.',
 'Click the run named `student-NN`. The **Overview** tab shows who created it, its status, its tags and the registered model with its version.',
 'Click the **Model metrics** tab. The `auc` chart shows the held-out score that Part 6 printed.',
]),
('figure','shots/13b_tools_mlflow.png','The MLflow tile under Tools & Frameworks.'),
('figure','shots/13_mlflow_experiments.png','MLflow Experiments. Search for your student number and open your experiment.'),
('figure','shots/14_mlflow_run.png','The run Overview. Tags, source, the logged model and the registered model version are all recorded.'),
('figure','shots/14b_mlflow_metrics.png','The Model metrics tab with the auc value.'),
('p','Compare your AUC with the other participants by opening their experiments the same way. With one dominant signal, hyperparameters move it by a fraction of a percent.'),
('note','Note','To create a second version, change `max_depth`, `n_estimators` or `learning_rate` in Part 6 and rerun Parts 6 to 8. The registry shows version 2 beside version 1. Do not expect large gains: better features beat better hyperparameters almost always.'),

('h2','Stage 6 - Measure CUDA-X and write the value'),
('h3','Part 9 - The same model on cuML'),
('p','cuML is a GPU implementation of the scikit-learn API. The cell trains a RandomForest with identical parameters twice, scikit-learn on the CPU cores the notebook has and cuML on the GPU, checks that the two AUCs agree, and only then compares the clock. A fast wrong answer is worth nothing, so accuracy is checked first.'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 9')),
('code','scikit-learn RandomForest :   4.02 s   AUC 0.8668   (4 CPU cores)\ncuML RandomForest         :   1.69 s   AUC 0.8659   (GPU)\n\n  AUC gap  : 0.0009   (same model within noise)\n  SPEED-UP : 2.4x   (4 CPU cores  ->  1 GPU)'),
('figure','shots/15a_part9.png','Part 9 output on the reference L40S run. Accuracy agrees, then the clock is compared against the four CPU cores of the notebook.'),
('p','Expected: two AUCs within 0.001 of each other, and a speed-up line that names the CPU core count it was measured against. That named comparison is the number you may quote. Vendor headline figures are ceilings measured on chosen workloads.'),
('h3','Part 10 - Look-alike search with cuVS'),
('p','A different question a retention team asks: who looks like the people who left? Every subscriber becomes a point in 19-dimensional space, and for each held-out subscriber the cell finds the ten most similar subscribers in the training set. The share of those neighbours that churned is a model-free churn score. The search runs with scikit-learn brute force on the CPU and cuVS brute force on the GPU, and recall between the two is checked before timing.'),
('code',cell('---\n# Part 10')),
('code','scikit-learn brute force :   1.85 s   50,000 queries x 150,000 vectors x 19 dims   (4 CPU cores)\ncuVS brute force         :   0.05 s   (GPU)\n\n  recall@10 vs CPU : 0.9997   (same neighbours)\n  SPEED-UP        : 37.5x\n\nlook-alike churn score AUC : 0.8143   (no model was trained for this)\ntrained XGBoost AUC        : 0.8712'),
('figure','shots/15_part10.png','Part 10 output. Recall checked, then the measured speed-up and the look-alike score.'),
('p','Similarity alone gets most of the way, and the model earns the rest. The model is what you register, version and serve. Similarity is what you use in a meeting. Exact brute-force search is the right tool at 150,000 vectors of 19 dimensions. cuVS\'s approximate indexes such as CAGRA exist for millions of embedding-sized vectors, and at this shape they are slower and less accurate.'),
('note','Important','A cuVS brute-force index references the GPU array it was built on without copying it. The cell keeps that array in a variable until the search completes. If the array is released early the search reads freed memory and recall silently drops to zero.'),
('h3','Part 11 - From your numbers to a value statement'),
('p','The final cell collects every number you measured into one table. It is the raw material for the last deliverable.'),
('code','YOUR MEASURED RESULTS  (student 07)\n\n  Columnar storage (Spark → Parquet)  849 MB → 232 MB CSV→Parquet             3.66x smaller\n  Partition pruning                   180 day folders written, 30 read        83% of files never opened\n  Absence as signal (LEFT join)       3,436 zero-activity subscribers kept    63% of them churned vs 12% overall\n  cuDF squash (20M → 200k)            CPU 0.39 s  GPU 0.03 s                  12.0x\n  cuML RandomForest                   CPU 4.02 s  GPU 1.69 s  (4 cores)       2.4x\n  cuVS look-alike search              CPU 1.85 s  GPU 0.05 s                  37.5x\n  Model quality                       XGBoost AUC 0.8712; look-alike AUC 0.8143\n  Reproducibility                     student-07-churn registered in MLflow   params + metric + model, versioned'),
('p','Write three value statements from your own run, each in three lines: the technical result, what it means for the work, and why the customer cares. Say only the third line to a customer, and have the first ready when they ask compared with what. At least one statement must be about a data-engineering result such as storage, federation or validation, not acceleration.'),

